---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import { openAiDevDayArticle as article } from '../../data/writing';
---
<BaseLayout title={`${article.title} | Haseeb Sultan`} description={article.description} article={{ published: article.published, image: article.image, headline: article.title }}>
  <Header />
  <main id="main" class="shell article-page">
    <article aria-labelledby="article-title">
      <figure class="article-cover">
        <img src={article.image} alt={article.imageAlt} width={article.imageWidth} height={article.imageHeight} fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
      </figure>

      <header class="article-heading reading-column">
        <a href="/writing/" class="article-back">← All writing</a>
        <p class="eyebrow">{article.category}</p>
        <h1 id="article-title">{article.title}</h1>
        <p class="article-deck">{article.description}</p>
        <p class="article-byline">Haseeb Sultan <span aria-hidden="true">·</span> <time datetime={article.published}>September 30, 2026</time></p>
      </header>

      <div class="article-body reading-column">
        <p class="article-lead">What if your AI did not just wait for your prompts, but worked quietly in the background around the clock—managing your calendar, updating slide decks, and resolving code bugs before you even woke up?</p>
        <p>Welcome to the era of <strong>agentic AI</strong>. OpenAI DevDay 2026 presented a major shift from conversational tools toward autonomous agents that collaborate, research, and build alongside us.</p>

        <aside class="learning-objectives" aria-labelledby="learning-objectives-title">
          <h2 id="learning-objectives-title">Learning Objectives</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>Understand Dots and how they represent a new form factor for autonomous AI agents.</li>
            <li>Explore the collaborative capabilities of ChatGPT Space for teams and agents.</li>
            <li>Compare GPT 6.1 sol, UltraFast, and the Decisions API.</li>
            <li>Identify key developer upgrades, including Codex in the Cloud and Private Tension.</li>
          </ul>
        </aside>

        <nav class="article-contents" aria-label="In this article">
          <p>In this article</p>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#dots-and-space">Meet Your Dot and ChatGPT Space</a></li>
            <li><a href="#models">Supercharged Models</a></li>
            <li><a href="#research-intern">The Rise of the AI Research Intern</a></li>
            <li><a href="#developer-ecosystem">The Next-Generation Developer Ecosystem</a></li>
            <li><a href="#key-takeaways">Key Takeaways</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <section aria-labelledby="dots-and-space">
          <h2 id="dots-and-space">Meet Your Dot and ChatGPT Space</h2>
          <p>Dots are not standard chatbots. Think of them as always-on virtual chiefs of staff. Powered by the Astra model, a Dot learns how you work over time and where you want to focus your attention. It can monitor bug reports, test code in draft pull requests, book plane tickets, or coordinate with vendors when plans go awry.</p>
          <p>Instead of telling your AI every individual step, you can delegate ambitious, multi-step projects. Dots have their own isolated cloud computers where they can write and test code. In the future, users will also be able to call them directly or exchange text messages with them.</p>
          <p>OpenAI introduced <strong>ChatGPT Space</strong> to make this collaboration seamless. It is a shared workspace where humans and Dots interact on the same page. A team can tag a Dot to track project dependencies, generate dynamic bar charts, or pull information from Slack channels in real time. For larger organizations, Specialist Dots can act as dedicated virtual teammates for intensive workflows.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="models">
          <h2 id="models">Supercharged Models: GPT 6.1 sol, UltraFast, and the Decisions API</h2>
          <p>OpenAI expanded its model lineup around three core measures: intelligence, speed, and cost. Each offering targets a different kind of work.</p>

          <div class="model-grid">
            <article class="model-card model-card-sol">
              <p class="model-label">Intelligence and value</p>
              <h3>GPT 6.1 sol</h3>
              <p>Near-frontier intelligence at a lower cost for long-running tasks, context reuse, iteration, and complex project workflows.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="model-card model-card-fast">
              <p class="model-label">Raw speed</p>
              <h3>UltraFast</h3>
              <p>Up to 300 tokens per second for live coding, real-time interaction, and near-instantaneous generation.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="model-card model-card-decisions">
              <p class="model-label">Structured choices</p>
              <h3>Decisions API</h3>
              <p>Sub-second responses for applications that select from a predefined set of options.</p>
            </article>
          </div>

          <h3>GPT 6.1 sol: The efficient daily driver</h3>
          <p>GPT 6.1 sol offers near-frontier intelligence at a fraction of the cost. It is designed for long tasks, making it more economical to reuse context, iterate on solutions, trace code dependencies, write pull requests, and plan multi-step projects.</p>

          <h3>UltraFast: Real-time generation</h3>
          <p>UltraFast produces up to 300 tokens per second across the API, ChatGPT, and Codex. Its raw output velocity is suited to real-time interactivity, live coding, reactive voice experiences, and the creation or manipulation of complex 3D environments.</p>

          <h3>Decisions API: Sub-second structured decisions</h3>
          <p>The Decisions API focuses the model on a predefined set of options to deliver structured responses with sub-second latency. This makes it useful for automated decision workflows and other applications where rapid, constrained selection matters more than open-ended generation.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="research-intern">
          <h2 id="research-intern">The Rise of the AI Research Intern</h2>
          <p>In 2025, OpenAI predicted that it would achieve the first autonomous AI research intern within a year. That milestone is now presented as a reality.</p>
          <p>Advanced post-training models excel at mathematics, coding, and computer use. Rather than only assisting researchers, they can run in continuous loops to train and improve future AI systems, refine safety filters, and reduce mistakes while navigating desktop applications.</p>
          <p>This virtual intern now carries out more than one-third of day-long research tasks without human intervention. The impact is already extending beyond software: these models have helped solve more than 100 longstanding mathematics problems, accelerate the synthesis of new antibiotics, and advance robotic manufacturing.</p>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="developer-ecosystem">
          <h2 id="developer-ecosystem">The Next-Generation Developer Ecosystem</h2>
          <p>OpenAI is making the tools it uses internally to build ChatGPT and Codex available to developers.</p>
          <div class="developer-list">
            <article>
              <h3>Codex in the Cloud</h3>
              <p>Developers can launch ambitious, cross-device tasks. A coding job can begin on a phone and continue in the cloud while Codex works through a complex revision, such as rewriting an entire backend in Rust.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Computer Use API</h3>
              <p>Agents can interact directly with computers by opening browsers, navigating pages, and carrying out workflows that resemble the work of a manual software tester.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>OpenAI Private Tension</h3>
              <p>This secure framework combines frontier-model capabilities with zero-data retention. Enterprise customer content is not stored on OpenAI servers, establishing a stronger standard for private AI workloads.</p>
            </article>
            <article>
              <h3>Monetization and Distribution</h3>
              <p>With Sign in with ChatGPT, people can access third-party applications using their existing ChatGPT plan tokens. This can reduce API-hosting costs for developers and make new software easier for consumers to try.</p>
            </article>
          </div>
        </section>

        <section aria-labelledby="key-takeaways">
          <h2 id="key-takeaways">Key Takeaways</h2>
          <ul class="takeaways">
            <li><strong>Dots and Space:</strong> AI is moving from passive chatbots to always-on agents that run on cloud computers and collaborate directly with teams.</li>
            <li><strong>Advanced models:</strong> GPT 6.1 sol focuses on cost-effective intelligence, UltraFast on high-speed generation, and the Decisions API on near-instant constrained choices.</li>
            <li><strong>Autonomous researchers:</strong> AI research agents can complete a growing share of long technical tasks while accelerating progress in mathematics, chemistry, and robotics.</li>
            <li><strong>Developer cloud upgrades:</strong> Codex in the Cloud, the Computer Use API, and Private Tension extend agentic capabilities while addressing workflow and privacy needs.</li>
            <li><strong>Simplified distribution:</strong> Sign in with ChatGPT gives third-party developers a new way to reach users without requiring them to manage separate model access.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <a href="/writing/" class="text-link">Explore more writing →</a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-page { padding-block: 40px 76px; }
  .article-cover img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: 8px; background: white; }
  .reading-column { max-width: 760px; margin-inline: auto; }
  .article-heading { padding-top: 36px; }
  .article-back { display: inline-flex; align-items: center; min-height: 44px; color: var(--color-accent); font-size: 14px; margin-bottom: 22px; }
  .article-heading h1 { font-size: clamp(34px, 5vw, 54px); line-height: 1.15; margin-top: 16px; letter-spacing: -.035em; }
  .article-deck { font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-muted); margin-top: 24px; }
  .article-byline { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 8px; font-size: 14px; color: var(--color-muted); padding-block: 22px 28px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-line); }
  .article-body { font-size: 17px; line-height: 1.9; padding-top: 32px; overflow-wrap: break-word; }
  .article-body p + p { margin-top: 20px; }
  .article-body h2 { font-size: clamp(26px, 4vw, 32px); line-height: 1.35; margin-block: 48px 20px; letter-spacing: -.025em; }
  .article-body h3 { font-size: 22px; line-height: 1.45; margin-block: 30px 14px; }
  .article-body ul, .article-body ol { padding-inline-start: 24px; margin-block: 20px; }
  .article-body ul { list-style: disc; }
  .article-body ol { list-style: decimal; }
  .article-body li + li { margin-top: 12px; }
  .article-body a:not(.text-link) { color: var(--color-accent); text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 3px; }
  .article-lead { font-size: 20px; line-height: 1.75; color: #354358; }
  .learning-objectives { margin-block: 32px; padding: 24px 28px; border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; background: #f2f5fa; }
  .learning-objectives h2 { margin: 0; font-size: 21px; }
  .learning-objectives ul { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .article-contents { margin-block: 32px; padding: 24px; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: 8px; background: white; font-size: 15px; }
  .article-contents > p { font-weight: 600; }
  .article-contents ol { margin-bottom: 0; }
  .model-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 14px; margin-block: 28px 36px; }
  .model-card { padding: 20px; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-top-width: 3px; border-radius: 8px; background: white; }
  .model-card-sol { border-top-color: #cf9d3d; }
  .model-card-fast { border-top-color: #22a4b4; }
  .model-card-decisions { border-top-color: #b02b9e; }
  .model-card .model-label { margin: 0 0 10px; color: var(--color-muted); font: 11px/1.4 var(--font-mono); letter-spacing: .04em; text-transform: uppercase; }
  .model-card h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 18px; }
  .model-card h3 + p { margin-top: 10px; color: var(--color-muted); font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.65; }
  .developer-list { display: grid; gap: 14px; margin-block: 28px 34px; }
  .developer-list article { padding: 22px 24px; border: 1px solid var(--color-line); border-radius: 8px; background: white; }
  .developer-list h3 { margin: 0; font-size: 19px; }
  .developer-list h3 + p { margin-top: 8px; color: var(--color-muted); font-size: 15px; line-height: 1.75; }
  .takeaways { padding: 24px 24px 24px 46px !important; border: 1px solid #d8e1ef; border-radius: 8px; background: #f4f7fb; }
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .article-page { padding-block: 24px 48px; }
    .article-heading { padding-top: 24px; }
    .article-deck, .article-lead { font-size: 18px; }
    .article-body { font-size: 16px; }
    .learning-objectives, .article-contents { padding: 20px; }
    .model-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
  }
</style>
